\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Use $R(a)=\prod_{p\mid a}p$ for the radical of a positive integer.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every $1\le a<b$ with $R(a)=R(b)$, must there be a prime $p$ satisfying $a<p<b$? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must two different positive integers with the same prime divisors have a prime strictly between them?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8700011 (AMR-086-0011). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Michel Waldschmidt, Open Diophantine Problems, arXiv:math/0312440v2 (24 January 2004), Conjecture 2.3. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}.
\end{description}
